Please perform a complete audit of the voting functionality that we have already implemented and make sure it covers all required UAT scenarios.

Important business requirement:
- Every eligible team member should be able to vote for ANY item, not only P1 items.
- Voting must work for P1, P2, P3, P4 and P5 items.
- The current voting model is binary:
  1. Add Vote
  2. Remove Existing Vote
- Do NOT implement true negative/downvote voting.
- Do NOT add a separate downvote count.
- Preserve the existing database schema, API contracts and permission model unless a real defect requires a minimal change.

Current expected UI:
- Items table should use one combined voting control.
- Item-detail page should contain a dedicated Voting section/card.
- Both views should use the same reusable voting component and business logic.

Your task:

1. Inspect the complete existing voting implementation:
   - Items table voting UI
   - Item-detail voting UI
   - Reusable voting component
   - Vote API endpoints
   - Database queries
   - Permission checks
   - Vote state management
   - Optimistic updates
   - Error handling
   - Existing unit/integration tests

2. Compare the existing implementation against all scenarios listed below.

3. For every scenario:
   - Mark whether it is already covered.
   - Identify the relevant component, API or test file.
   - If the scenario is missing or partially implemented, add the required implementation.
   - Do not rewrite working functionality unnecessarily.

Required scenarios to validate and cover:

A. Role and permission scenarios

1. Super Admin can vote on an eligible item according to the existing permission model.
2. A normal user who is a member of the item’s team can vote.
3. A user who is not a member of the team cannot vote.
4. An unauthorized user must not be able to vote by directly calling the API.
5. Read-only users should see vote totals but should not see an active voting action.
6. Permission checks must exist on the backend, not only in the UI.
7. Voting permissions must remain consistent on both the items table and item-detail page.

B. Priority and item scenarios

8. Voting must work for P1 items.
9. Voting must work for P2 items.
10. Voting must work for P3 items.
11. Voting must work for P4 items.
12. Voting must work for P5 items.
13. Voting must not contain any condition that restricts it only to P1.
14. Verify expected behaviour for active, completed, solved, converted, merged or archived items.
15. If voting is not allowed for a particular item status, show a clear disabled/read-only state instead of silently failing.

C. Vote lifecycle scenarios

16. A user can add one vote to an item.
17. The vote count increases by exactly one.
18. A user cannot create duplicate votes for the same item.
19. Clicking Vote repeatedly must not create multiple database records.
20. A user who has already voted can remove their vote.
21. Removing a vote decreases the count by exactly one.
22. A user cannot remove another user’s vote.
23. Removing a vote that does not exist should not produce a negative count or inconsistent state.
24. Vote state must persist after page refresh.
25. Vote state must persist after logout and login.
26. Multiple users voting on the same item should update the total correctly.
27. The total vote count must never become negative.
28. Concurrent vote requests must not create duplicate records or incorrect totals.

D. Items-table UI scenarios

29. Show one compact combined voting control inside the Votes column.
30. Show the current total vote count.
31. Show a clear selected/highlighted state when the current user has voted.
32. When the user has not voted, show the Vote action.
33. When the user has voted, show the Remove Vote action.
34. Do not display the Remove Vote action as a true downvote.
35. Tooltips should clearly say:
    - “Vote for this item”
    - “Remove your vote”
36. Disable the control while the vote request is processing.
37. Prevent repeated rapid clicks.
38. The control must be keyboard accessible.
39. Add appropriate aria-labels.
40. The control must remain usable on small screens and responsive layouts.
41. Existing table sorting, filtering, pagination and row selection must not break.

E. Item-detail page scenarios

42. Add or verify a dedicated Voting section/card.
43. Show the total number of votes.
44. Show whether the current user has voted.
45. Show Vote or Remove Vote based on the current user’s state.
46. Reuse the same shared VotingControl component used by the table.
47. If the user cannot vote, show the total in read-only mode.
48. For unauthorized users show a clear message such as:
    “Only eligible team members can vote on this item.”
49. The voting card must follow the existing design system.
50. It must support dark mode and responsive layouts if the application already supports them.

F. Synchronization scenarios

51. Voting from the items table must be reflected on the item-detail page.
52. Voting from the item-detail page must be reflected on the items table.
53. Both views must show the same total count.
54. Both views must show the same current-user vote state.
55. Navigation between list and detail must not show stale vote data.
56. After refresh, both views must load the latest server state.

G. Loading and error scenarios

57. Show a loading/disabled state while the vote API request is running.
58. Use optimistic updates only if the existing architecture supports them safely.
59. If the request fails, rollback the optimistic UI state.
60. Show the application’s existing toast/error message.
61. Network failure must not leave an incorrect vote count.
62. A 401 response should follow the existing authentication handling.
63. A 403 response should show permission feedback.
64. A 404 response should handle deleted or unavailable items safely.
65. The UI should not crash if vote data is null, undefined or temporarily unavailable.

H. Data integrity scenarios

66. Enforce one vote per user per item.
67. Verify whether a unique database constraint already exists.
68. If it does not exist, do not immediately modify the schema. First report the risk and add the safest minimal protection according to the project architecture.
69. Vote totals should be calculated from reliable server data.
70. Do not trust a client-supplied user ID or vote count.
71. Verify that all API operations use the authenticated user.
72. Ensure the user cannot vote for an item they are not allowed to access.

I. Regression scenarios

73. Existing item creation and editing must continue to work.
74. Existing team permissions must continue to work.
75. Existing filters and sorting must continue to work.
76. Existing item-detail tabs must continue to work.
77. No database schema, role behaviour or API response should be changed unnecessarily.
78. No unrelated files should be modified.

